        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Prambanan</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 856 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Likely begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rakai-pikatan">Rakai Pikatan</a> and inaugurated by his successor King Lokapala of the Mataram kingdom, then expanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balitung">Balitung</a> Maha Sambu and later Mataram kings such as Daksa and Tulodong</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple compound on a square plan of three walled zones, once with 240 temple structures; the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple is 47 metres tall and 34 metres wide, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> temples are 33 metres tall</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/special-region-of-yogyakarta">Special Region of Yogyakarta</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, about 17 kilometres northeast of Yogyakarta, on the boundary with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a></span></div>
