    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The compound was likely begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rakai-pikatan">Rakai Pikatan</a> and inaugurated by King Lokapala, and the Shivagrha inscription of 856 describes a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple compound resembling Prambanan.</li><li>It served as the royal temple of the Mataram kingdom, was abandoned around the middle of the 10th century, and fell into ruin after a major earthquake in the 16th century.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonial restoration began in the 1930s, the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple was completed in 1953 and the central temples by 1993, but only six of the 224 pervara temples have been fully rebuilt.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>According to the Shivagrha inscription, a public water project moved the course of a river near the temple during its construction; the river is identified as the Opak, which now runs along the western side of the compound.</p></aside>
    </section>

