    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">856</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple was inaugurated, according to the Shivagrha inscription issued by King Lokapala</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">930s</span><span class="rf-text">The Javanese court was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-sindok">Mpu Sindok</a>, and the temples were abandoned around the middle of the 10th century</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">16th century</span><span class="rf-text">The temples collapsed in a major earthquake</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1733</span><span class="rf-text">Cornelis Antonie Lons, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a> employee, gave the first report on the ruins in his journal</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1805</span><span class="rf-text">Nicolaus Engelhard commissioned H.C. Cornelius to clear, measure and draw the site, the first effort to study and restore it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1953</span><span class="rf-text">The reconstruction of the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple was completed and inaugurated by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1993</span><span class="rf-text">The main temples of the central zone were complete and inaugurated by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2019</span><span class="rf-text">The Abhiṣeka ceremony was held for the first time, to cleanse and sanctify the temple as a focus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> worship</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/borobudur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/borobudur.jpg" alt="Borobudur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pradaksina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Heri nugroho, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Prambanan was built in the 9th century in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kewu-plain">Prambanan Plain</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, where the court and urban centre of the Mataram kingdom lay nearby. The first building was completed in the mid-9th century. It was likely started by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rakai-pikatan">Rakai Pikatan</a> and inaugurated by his successor King Lokapala. A short red-paint script reading "pikatan" on a finial of the balustrade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple is taken to confirm that Pikatan began the work.</p><p>Some historians who hold to the dual dynasty theory suggest that the temple was meant as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Sanjaya dynasty's answer to the Buddhist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty">Sailendra dynasty</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> and Sewu temples nearby, and to mark the Sanjaya return to power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a> after almost a century of Sailendra domination. Whatever its motive, the building of this large Hindu temple signified a shift of the Mataram court's patronage from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a> to Shaivite Hinduism. The Shivagrha inscription of 856, issued by Lokapala, describes a Shiva temple compound resembling Prambanan, inaugurated on 12 November 856; the temple's original name was Shiva-grha, the House of Shiva, or Shiva-laya, the Realm of Shiva.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ramayana"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramayana.jpg" alt="Ramayana" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C4%81ma_slays_R%C4%81va%E1%B9%87a.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scribes and painters employed by the Kingdom of Mewar., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The compound is dedicated to the Trimūrti, the expression of God as Creator (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>), Preserver (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>) and Destroyer (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>). Its plan is square, with three zones, each walled and pierced by four gates. The outer zone, once about 390 metres per side, has almost wholly vanished, and its original function is unknown. The middle zone, about 225 metres per side, holds four rows of 44, 52, 60 and 68 pervara (ancillary) temples, 224 in all. The inner zone, the holiest, is a raised platform with eight main shrines and eight small ones.</p><p>The three main shrines face east: Shiva in the centre, Vishnu to the north and Brahma to the south, with a vahana temple in front of each for the god's mount: the bull Nandi, the kite Garuda and the swan Hamsa. The Shiva temple, 47 metres tall and 34 metres wide, has five chambers; the central one holds a three-metre statue of Shiva Mahadeva, and the others hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agastya">Agastya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a> as slayer of the Bull demon. Narrative reliefs along the balustrades tell the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, from the Shiva temple into the Brahma temple, and the stories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavata-purana">Bhagavata Purana</a> on the Vishnu temple. The lower outer walls carry niches with a lion flanked by kalpataru trees, called the Prambanan panel.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The temple was expanded extensively by King Lokapala and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balitung">Balitung</a> Maha Sambu, and later Mataram kings such as Daksa and Tulodong added hundreds of pervara temples around the chief temple. According to the Shivagrha inscription, a public water project changed the course of a river near the temple during construction. The river, identified as the Opak, was cut along a north to south axis beside the outer wall, and its old course was filled in and levelled to make room for the rows of ancillary temples. Experts suggest the shift was meant to protect the compound from lahar from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-merapi">Mount Merapi</a>.</p><p>The design follows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> architectural tradition based on Vastu Shastra, with a mandala plan, and was meant to mimic Meru, the holy mountain, the abode of the gods and the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>. The three zones of the plan and of each temple correspond to the realms of Bhurloka, Bhuvarloka and Svarloka. During restoration a well 5.75 metres deep was found under the centre of the Shiva temple, holding a stone casket on a pile of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a>, earth and burned animal bones; the casket held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> sheets, 20 coins, jewels and 12 gold leaves cut in shapes including a turtle and a Nāga serpent.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Prambanan was the royal temple of the Kingdom of Mataram, and most of the state's religious ceremonies and sacrifices were conducted there. Scholars estimate that at the height of the kingdom hundreds of brahmins with their disciples lived within the outer wall. Some archaeologists propose that the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> in the central chamber was modelled after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balitung">Balitung</a>, as a depiction of his deified self after death; some historians likewise believe that showing Shiva as Mahadeva was meant to personify Balitung as Shiva's reincarnation. The meaning of the pervara rows is debated: some believed they were offered by regional rulers and nobles as a sign of submission, some that the rows matched four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, and others that they were simply meditation places for priests and worship places for devotees.</p><p>After the temples ceased to be an important centre of worship, local Javanese people still knew the ruins but not which kings had built them, and they explained the site with the Rara Jonggrang legend. In it Bandung Bondowoso, helped by spirits, built 999 temples in one night to win the princess; she tricked the spirits into fleeing at a false dawn, and he cursed her into stone, becoming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a> statue in the north chamber, still called Rara Jonggrang.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles.jpg" alt="Stamford Raffles" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Stamford Raffles</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Francis_Joseph_-_Sir_Thomas_Stamford_Bingley_Raffles.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Francis Joseph, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After about 80 years of use and expansion, the temples were abandoned near the middle of the 10th century. In the 930s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-sindok">Mpu Sindok</a> moved the Javanese court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>. The reason is not clear; the 1006 eruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-merapi">Mount Merapi</a> or a power struggle may have caused the shift. The temples collapsed in a major earthquake in the 16th century. Cornelis Antonie Lons made the first report in 1733, and in 1755 the ruins and the Opak River were used to mark the boundary between the Yogyakarta and Surakarta Sultanates. Nicolaus Engelhard had the site cleared and drawn from 1805, and Colin Mackenzie, a surveyor for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Thomas Stamford Raffles</a>, came upon the temples by chance in 1811.</p><p>The ruins then stayed neglected for decades. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> residents carried off sculptures as garden ornaments, villagers took foundation stones for building, and half-hearted excavations in the 1880s facilitated looting, as many sculptures were taken away for collections. The Dutch colonial government began reconstruction in 1918, with proper restoration from 1930 by the anastylosis method, which reuses the original blocks. It was halted by the economic crisis, the Pacific War and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-revolution">Indonesian National Revolution</a>, and resumed in 1949.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angkor-wat.jpg" alt="Angkor Wat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angkor_Wat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Prambanan is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> and the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and the second largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple was completed in 1953 and inaugurated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> temple was rebuilt in 1978 to 1987 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> temple in 1982 to 1991; by 1993 the main central temples were complete. Because much of the original stonework was stolen and reused elsewhere, the government decided to rebuild a shrine only where at least 75% of its masonry survives. Of the 224 pervara temples, six were fully reconstructed by February 2023; the rest are mostly foundations or scattered stones, and rebuilding them all would take at least 200 years at 8 to 12 months each.</p><p>Since the 1960s a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> ballet, the Javanese court dance, has been performed on full moon nights on stages west of the Opak River. After the 1990s reconstruction, Balinese and Javanese Hindu communities revived ceremonies there such as Galungan, Tawur Kesanga and Nyepi. A grand Abhiṣeka ceremony in November 2019, the first held there, was meant to cleanse and sanctify the temple and restore its function as a focus of Hindu worship. The 2006 Yogyakarta earthquake damaged the site, which reopened some weeks later.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Prambanan is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple compound in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, begun by the Mataram court in the 9th century and dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>. It was abandoned, collapsed in an earthquake and was rebuilt from the 1930s by anastylosis; it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</em></p>

